## Lessons Learned

This lab demonstrated that successful network communication requires more than simply assigning IP addresses to devices.

The subnet mask determines whether a destination is considered local or remote. Devices on the same subnet can communicate through Layer 2 switching, while communication between different IP networks requires Layer 3 routing.

The lab also demonstrated how encapsulation allows each layer of the TCP/IP model to add the information necessary to deliver data. Transport information is encapsulated inside an IP packet, which is then encapsulated inside an Ethernet frame before being transmitted through the physical network.

Finally, the lab reinforced the relationship between **subnetting, routing, and ARP**. The routing table determines how a destination network can be reached, while ARP determines the MAC address required for local Ethernet delivery.

Understanding how these processes work together provides the foundation for later CCNA topics including **routing, VLANs, inter-VLAN routing, static routes, OSPF, ACLs, and network troubleshooting**.
